# Username and password sign-in

This guide adds accounts to a Howdy app with
[Howdy Auth](/docs/auth/overview). By the end, a visitor can register,
choose a username, and sign in with that username and a password.

One thing to know before starting: Howdy Auth identifies an account by its
*email address*, and registration confirms the address by emailing a token.
That address is also how a forgotten password is recovered. So a username
does not replace the email address. It is a unique
[field](/docs/auth/fields) on the user, and signing in with it is one small
route of your own. The guide builds both.

It assumes a project made with `howdy new my_app`, on SQLite. See
[Getting started](/docs/guides/getting-started) if you do not have one.

## Install

Add Howdy Auth and Howdy Database to the dependencies in `gleam.toml`:

```toml
howdy_auth = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "auth" }
howdy_database = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "database" }
```

`howdy_database` provides `howdy/migration`, which runs the auth
migrations.

Then download them:

```sh
gleam deps download
```

## Create the tables

Howdy Auth brings its own [migrations](/docs/auth/migrations). In
`src/my_app/platform/database.gleam`, add a function that runs them:

```gleam
import howdy/auth
import howdy/migration
import howdy/service

pub fn migrate_auth(db: Repo) -> Result(Nil, String) {
  migration.run(db, [auth.schema()])
  |> result.map_error(service.message)
}
```

## Configure auth

Create `src/my_app/accounts/identity.gleam`. It builds the one `Auth` value
the rest of the app shares:

```gleam
import gleam/io
import gloo/repo.{type Repo}
import howdy/auth.{type Auth}
import howdy/auth/secret

pub fn new(
  db: Repo,
  origin: String,
  deliver: fn(auth.Delivery) -> Result(Nil, Nil),
) -> Auth {
  let assert Ok(identity) = auth.new(repo: db, origin: origin, deliver: deliver)
  let identity = auth.allow_registration(identity)
  let assert Ok(identity) = auth.with_passwords(identity)
  identity
}

/// For development only: print the token instead of emailing it.
pub fn print_email(delivery: auth.Delivery) -> Result(Nil, Nil) {
  io.println(
    "Token for " <> delivery.email <> ": " <> secret.reveal(delivery.token),
  )
  Ok(Nil)
}
```

- `allow_registration` lets visitors create accounts.
- `with_passwords` turns on [passwords](/docs/auth/passwords). Without it
  there are only email tokens.
- `deliver` is the function that sends email. Printing the token is fine on
  your own machine. Before you deploy, replace `print_email` with one that
  really sends: see [Sending the email](/docs/auth/signing-in).

## Mount it

In `src/my_app.gleam`, take the `Auth` as an argument to `app`, and mount
the JSON API and the starter pages:

```gleam
import howdy/auth.{type Auth}
import howdy/auth/pages
import howdy/auth/routes

pub fn app(db: Repo, identity: Auth) -> howdy.App {
  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.controller(routes.api(identity, at: "/api/auth"))
  |> howdy.controller(pages.routes(identity, at: "/auth", api_at: "/api/auth"))
  |> howdy.controller(todos.controller(db))
  |> howdy.controller(site.controller(db))
  // The root static controller is a catch-all, so mount it last.
  |> howdy.controller(static.serve("/", from: "priv/public"))
}
```

Pass it through `configured` in the same way, then build it in `main`:

```gleam
pub fn main() -> Nil {
  load_environment()
  let db = database()
  let assert Ok(_) = database.migrate(db)

  // Auth gets a connection of its own to the same file.
  let auth_db = database()
  let assert Ok(_) = database.migrate_auth(auth_db)
  let identity =
    identity.new(auth_db, "http://localhost:8789", identity.print_email)

  let assert Ok(_) = configured(db, identity) |> howdy.start
  process.sleep_forever()
}
```

Two details matter here:

- The origin must be the address in the browser's address bar, exactly.
  Open the app at `http://localhost:8789`, not `http://127.0.0.1:8789`, or
  sign-in is refused with `403`. In production it is your `https://`
  address; read it from the environment.
- Gloo gives each SQLite Repo one connection, so auth gets its own Repo.
  On PostgreSQL, pass the pool you already have.

The files in `dev/` and `test/` that call `app` need the new argument too.
The compiler will point at each of them.

## Register

Start the app with `gleam dev` and open
<http://localhost:8789/auth/password/register>. Enter an email address and
a password.

Passwords must be at least 15 characters, and common ones are refused, so
`password123456789` will not do. A few unrelated words work well.

The page asks for the token that was "emailed". Copy it from the terminal,
where `print_email` wrote it, and paste it in. The account now exists, and
the browser holds a session cookie. <http://localhost:8789/auth/account>
lists your sessions and signs you out.

This much is a complete email and password sign-in:
<http://localhost:8789/auth/password/login> takes the address and the
password. The rest of the guide adds the username.

## Declare the username

Create `src/my_app/accounts/username.gleam`:

```gleam
import gleam/list
import gleam/string
import howdy/auth/field

pub fn definition() -> field.Field(String) {
  field.text("username")
  |> field.unique
  |> field.check(fn(name) {
    let length = string.length(name)
    let allowed =
      string.to_graphemes(name)
      |> list.all(string.contains("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789_", _))
    case length >= 3 && length <= 30 && allowed {
      True -> Ok(Nil)
      False -> Error("must be 3 to 30 lowercase letters, digits or underscores")
    }
  })
}

/// Usernames are compared in lower case, without surrounding spaces.
pub fn normalize(name: String) -> String {
  string.lowercase(string.trim(name))
}
```

`field.unique` makes the database refuse a username somebody else holds,
even when two people ask for it at the same moment. The check keeps
usernames to a small alphabet, which also rules out an `@`: a username can
never be mistaken for an email address.

## Choose a username, and sign in with it

Create `src/my_app/accounts/accounts_controller.gleam`:

```gleam
import gleam/dynamic/decode
import gleam/json
import gleam/option
import gleam/result
import howdy/auth.{type Auth}
import howdy/auth/field
import howdy/auth/secret
import howdy/auth/user
import howdy/auth/users
import howdy/body
import howdy/context
import howdy/controller
import howdy/cookie
import howdy/guard
import howdy/service
import my_app/accounts/username

pub fn controller(identity: Auth) -> controller.Controller {
  controller.new("/api/accounts")
  |> controller.post("/username", choose_username(identity, _))
  |> controller.post("/login", login(identity, _))
  |> controller.get("/me", me(identity, _))
}

fn choose_username(identity: Auth, ctx: controller.Context) {
  use principal <- guard.require(ctx, auth.required(identity))
  use name <- body.json(
    ctx,
    decode.field("username", decode.string, decode.success),
  )
  users.update(
    identity,
    principal.user.id,
    [field.set(username.definition(), username.normalize(name))],
    by: user.Acting(principal),
  )
  |> service.respond(ctx, user.to_json)
}
```

`auth.required` is a [guard](/docs/controllers/guards), so only a signed-in
visitor reaches `users.update`, and they can only change their own
username, because the id comes from their session and not from the request.
A username that is taken answers `409`, and one that fails the check
answers `400` with the message.

Next, in the same file, the sign-in. Look the username up, then hand the
account's email address and the password to Howdy Auth:

```gleam
pub fn sign_in(
  identity: Auth,
  name: String,
  password: String,
  client: String,
) -> service.Result(auth.Session) {
  let name = username.normalize(name)
  use found <- result.try(users.find(
    identity,
    where: username.definition(),
    is: name,
  ))
  let email = case found {
    [user] -> user.email
    // Nobody has this username. Check the password anyway, against an
    // address that can never have an account.
    _ -> name <> "@username.invalid"
  }
  auth.login_password_from(identity, email, password, client)
}
```

Do not return early when the username is unknown. `login_password_from`
does the same slow password check whether or not the account exists, and
gives the same `401` either way. Sending unknown usernames through it keeps
that true for your route, so nobody can use the sign-in form to find out
which usernames are taken. It also means guesses against a username are
[slowed down](/docs/auth/passwords) just as guesses against an address are.

The route wraps that function. It sets the same cookie that Howdy Auth's
own routes set, so every guard in the app accepts the session:

```gleam
fn login(identity: Auth, ctx: controller.Context) {
  use _ <- guard.require(ctx, auth.check_origin(identity, _))
  use #(name, password) <- body.json(ctx, {
    use name <- decode.field("username", decode.string)
    use password <- decode.field("password", decode.string)
    decode.success(#(name, password))
  })
  let client = context.client_ip(ctx.request) |> option.unwrap("")
  case sign_in(identity, name, password, client) {
    Error(error) -> service.error_response(ctx, error)
    Ok(session) ->
      controller.json(ctx, user.to_json(session.user))
      |> cookie.set(
        auth.cookie_name(identity),
        secret.reveal(session.token),
        cookie.defaults()
          |> cookie.secure(auth.secure(identity))
          |> cookie.max_age(auth.policy(identity).session_seconds),
      )
  }
}
```

`auth.check_origin` refuses a sign-in posted from another site. `client` is
what the password limits count by; if the app runs
[behind a proxy](/docs/auth/administration), read the proxy's header
instead of the socket address.

Last, a route that shows who is signed in. Fields are not loaded with the
session, so it reads them:

```gleam
fn me(identity: Auth, ctx: controller.Context) {
  use principal <- guard.require(ctx, auth.required(identity))
  users.fields(identity, principal.user.id)
  |> service.respond(ctx, fn(data) {
    json.object([
      #("email", json.string(principal.user.email)),
      #(
        "username",
        json.nullable(
          field.get(data, username.definition()) |> option.from_result,
          json.string,
        ),
      ),
    ])
  })
}
```

Mount the controller in `app`, above the static files:

```gleam
|> howdy.controller(accounts.controller(identity))
```

## Try it

While you are still signed in from registering, choose a username from the
browser's console:

```javascript
await fetch('/api/accounts/username', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
  body: JSON.stringify({username: 'ada'}),
});
```

Sign out at `/auth/account`, then sign in with the username:

```javascript
const response = await fetch('/api/accounts/login', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
  body: JSON.stringify({
    username: 'ada',
    password: 'a long and unusual passphrase',
  }),
});
```

`/api/accounts/me` now answers with the email address and the username. A
real app would put a form in front of each of these calls: see
[Forms](/docs/controllers/forms) and
[Building a UI](/docs/guides/building-a-ui).

## Protect your pages

Anything that needs a signed-in visitor uses the same guard:

```gleam
controller.guarded("/dashboard", auth.required(identity))
|> controller.get("/", fn(ctx) {
  controller.text(ctx, "Hello, " <> ctx.guard.user.email)
})
|> controller.build()
```

A visitor who is not signed in gets `401`. Howdy Auth never redirects, so
sending them to your sign-in page is up to you.

## Forgotten passwords

A password is reset through the email address, which is why every account
has one. The visitor asks for a token at `/auth/login`, pastes it in, and
within ten minutes sets a new password at `/auth/account`. Doing so signs
them out everywhere else. [Passwords](/docs/auth/passwords) explains why
it works this way.

## Test it

The flow can be tested end to end with
[`howdy/testing`](/docs/guides/testing). Use an in-memory database, and a
`deliver` function that hands the token to the test:

```gleam
import gleam/erlang/process
import gleam/json
import gleam/list
import gloo/adapter/sqlite
import howdy/auth
import howdy/auth/secret
import howdy/testing
import my_app
import my_app/accounts/identity
import my_app/platform/database

const origin = "http://localhost:8789"

const password = "a long and unusual passphrase"

pub fn username_sign_in_test() {
  let assert Ok(db) = sqlite.start(sqlite.memory())
  let assert Ok(_) = database.migrate(db)
  let assert Ok(_) = database.migrate_auth(db)
  let inbox = process.new_subject()
  let identity =
    identity.new(db, origin, fn(delivery: auth.Delivery) {
      process.send(inbox, secret.reveal(delivery.token))
      Ok(Nil)
    })
  let app = my_app.app(db, identity)
  let cookie_name = auth.cookie_name(identity)

  // Register, and confirm the address with the token.
  let res =
    testing.post(
      "/api/auth/password/register",
      json.object([
        #("email", json.string("ada@example.com")),
        #("password", json.string(password)),
      ]),
    )
    |> testing.send(app)
  assert res.status == 202
  let assert Ok(token) = process.receive(inbox, 1000)
  let res =
    testing.post(
      "/api/auth/session",
      json.object([#("token", json.string(token))]),
    )
    |> testing.header("origin", origin)
    |> testing.send(app)
  let assert Ok(session) = list.key_find(testing.cookies(res), cookie_name)

  // Choose a username.
  let res =
    testing.post(
      "/api/accounts/username",
      json.object([#("username", json.string("Ada"))]),
    )
    |> testing.header("origin", origin)
    |> testing.cookie(cookie_name, session)
    |> testing.send(app)
  assert res.status == 200

  // Sign in with it.
  let login = fn(name, password) {
    testing.post(
      "/api/accounts/login",
      json.object([
        #("username", json.string(name)),
        #("password", json.string(password)),
      ]),
    )
    |> testing.header("origin", origin)
    |> testing.send(app)
  }
  assert login("ada", password).status == 200
  assert login("ada", "not the right passphrase").status == 401
  assert login("nobody", password).status == 401
}
```

A test shares one in-memory database between the app and auth, which is
fine because a test makes one request at a time. Password hashing is slow
on purpose, so keep the number of sign-ins in your tests small.

## Where next

- [Sessions and policy](/docs/auth/sessions) changes how long a session
  lasts and how short a password may be.
- [Roles and permissions](/docs/auth/roles) decides what each user may do.
- [User administration](/docs/auth/administration) creates users by
  invitation, suspends them, and explains the limits to set behind a
  proxy.
